@*
    The frame every page sits in - and the reason the mark is now everywhere.

    On the native head this bar was a plain filled dot for months, under a comment
    claiming it read as the same object as the hero. It did not. Here the header
    renders the SAME BrandMark component the hero does, at a smaller size, so the
    two cannot drift: one drawing, one set of numbers.

    The whole bar navigates home, not the 30px mark. A control the size of a
    full-stop is a control most people miss.
*@
@implements IDisposable
@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<div class="app">
    @*
        NO BAR ON HOME, because the native screen has none: HomeActivity builds a
        wordmark, not a Ui.HomeBar, and the circle owns the upper half. Every other
        screen calls Ui.HomeBar(this, "<name>"), so every other screen gets one.
    *@
    @if (ScreenName is not null)
    {
        <header class="app-bar" @onclick="GoHome" role="banner">
            <BrandMark Size="30" />
            <span class="app-name">Circle AI</span>
            <span class="app-where">@ScreenName</span>
        </header>
    }

    <main class="app-body @(ScreenName is null ? "app-body-full" : null)">
        @Body
    </main>
</div>

@code {
    /*
        THE NAME COMES FROM THE ROUTE, not from a parameter.

        Blazor cannot pass a value from a routed page up into its layout, and the
        alternatives - cascading the layout instance, or a state service - are a
        lot of plumbing that can fall out of step with where you actually are.
        Reading the path cannot: if the URL says /languages, the bar says
        Languages.
    */
    static readonly Dictionary<string, string> Names = new(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
    {
        ["languages"] = "Languages",
        ["abilities"] = "What it can do",
        ["device"] = "This device",
    };

    string? ScreenName { get; set; }

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Nav.LocationChanged += OnLocationChanged;
        Resolve();
    }

    void OnLocationChanged(object? sender, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing.LocationChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        Resolve();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    void Resolve()
    {
        var path = Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri);
        // Drop any query or fragment before matching, or "/languages?q=ja" misses.
        var cut = path.IndexOfAny(['?', '#']);
        if (cut >= 0) path = path[..cut];
        ScreenName = Names.TryGetValue(path.Trim('/'), out var name) ? name : null;
    }

    void GoHome() => Nav.NavigateTo("");

    public void Dispose() => Nav.LocationChanged -= OnLocationChanged;
}
